#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153061} :activity/id #uuid "610c3ee9-b5f3-57d8-b82e-cac1f007574a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048694 :topic/id #uuid "b52b86c6-5073-5d60-a17c-32a308489156" :topic/math-academy-id 2638} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153355 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to evaluate the line integral with respect to arc length\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_Cf(x,y)\\:\\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nfor some function $f(x,y),$ where $C$ is a curve in the $xy$-plane defined using plane polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nr = r(\\theta),\\qquad \\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2.\n$$\n\nTo calculate this line integral, we need to recall the following facts:\n\n- A polar curve is just a special type of parametric curve in the parameter $\\theta,$ where\n\n$$\nx = r(\\theta)\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r(\\theta)\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2.\n$$\n\n- The arc length element $\\textrm d s$ in plane polar coordinates is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above formulas into our line integral, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's go through an example in which we apply this result." :tutorial/id #uuid "f970309b-6c25-53f1-a3b5-b094ebe87109" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3987 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1d46b30d-17ce-5fe6-a7be-dcb1352a6151" :step/math-academy-id 23719 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153062}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152160 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151949 :question/id #uuid "287a493f-e988-5cef-b0d0-4205d1eaf74e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6313"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497344}] :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"} :step/id #uuid "70b202ae-ec41-5eae-8ae1-a2c1f7937280" :step/math-academy-id 23720 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153063}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153063 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152161 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151950 :question/id #uuid "63aa09fb-9839-5f73-9f98-2eb9784ead43" :question/math-academy-id "e-6314"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9964be68-9e22-5d98-b0f8-ae19b6e94c1a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455471}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Polar Form"} :step/id #uuid "f2968451-7379-5754-b957-4337d8ab3c16" :step/math-academy-id 23721 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153064}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152162 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151951 :question/id #uuid "00b29cf2-6a73-5fd6-b8bd-04ba8c5f1a46" :question/math-academy-id "e-6315"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6e8ead-8186-5afe-bd66-d11c55b65dd4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455509}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "15d98898-5aa7-5f1a-9c11-0b4e8416b8d1" :step/math-academy-id 23722}] :activity/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Polar Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152335}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483447} :activity/id #uuid "5612666d-368d-549d-8c98-b899d091fd25" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048694 :topic/id #uuid "b52b86c6-5073-5d60-a17c-32a308489156" :topic/math-academy-id 2638} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483435 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bba08e7-72f6-5628-a032-40fab3b1523b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342340} {:answer/id #uuid "966bb9f4-b258-530d-bda2-5cc45dd1bfc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343555} {:answer/id #uuid "da527ec0-d3e9-50cf-934b-d37d6ce14b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344156} {:answer/id #uuid "dc075c9d-4ff5-5e6a-83cc-7b9150d7aa65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344168} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3267aa-cefb-5be3-9449-fd0ab72c839c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344296}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344168} :answer-field/id #uuid "56b83e59-aa7d-5940-925e-575b2e63efa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fee02b99-a9f3-5976-9c5b-34d5a43071ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-76690" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta | \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta |=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ because $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta >0.$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-0-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0cb113d7-5c3c-54c0-89a9-97aef97d74cf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483445}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483445 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab50b4da-933b-580c-90e3-d57fe918ca3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283174} {:answer/id #uuid "e0621d5d-1546-515b-b160-26c36601cee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283647} {:answer/id #uuid "eac1d727-6b15-5ede-a524-9f9b5cca59df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283741} {:answer/id #uuid "f038538b-7314-51bc-a23b-a2dc236cb725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283787} {:answer/id #uuid "f848449c-f299-591e-af24-cf140841f848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283860}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283647} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1857575-157f-5eb5-ba46-57df0567255a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e623a86a-8a2d-5ca1-949a-2b47126336d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-165414" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )=-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "21aa696c-4f6f-58d7-9ba8-f3f7ce7733c1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483447 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c31779c1-61a9-5ddf-9ce4-388acac151e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393613} {:answer/id #uuid "b35b8c73-b6e1-55f6-a3a8-1f84f618409d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393614} {:answer/id #uuid "d14288db-cd96-5f69-9f29-fa682d61a4a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393615} {:answer/id #uuid "f83c51d2-6855-5059-8910-c179f8d7f6c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393616} {:answer/id #uuid "865a2778-efa4-5860-8c94-4fab14e1e597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393617}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393614} :answer-field/id #uuid "8116cea9-3c1b-5e81-a58c-b2147c4e208d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fda4f6af-c917-5961-9062-323f1cdf3aeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-76355" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{2})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2670f88c-dfda-5312-b900-fa039a6fdb8a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483462}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483462 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d839b3-7560-5512-ab94-65b25afb8af3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482817} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd0b7d8-497a-5308-9309-036b9effbae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482828} {:answer/id #uuid "11be0602-805c-5751-80e1-20619f10d315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482841} {:answer/id #uuid "1663c28c-4cc3-57a0-901a-0782cd1f5920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482854} {:answer/id #uuid "e62cdbaf-ad20-51f2-b72a-b2f5ab5a24eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483200}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482854} :answer-field/id #uuid "63f7e3dc-e6fb-53cc-b486-dbf54e61e1b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "80893bd5-f8f9-5d69-b7d0-36e40ddfd184" :question/math-academy-id "q-76572" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5da74330-e29e-5830-9670-415d6daf1e43" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483476}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483476 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5daa3e96-058a-548e-9727-56398515faab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343024} {:answer/id #uuid "65d6b8b8-c97c-5abc-aecb-7e85e4054fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343101} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb80056-6ab1-5d01-af98-8aaa5fd2fb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343301} {:answer/id #uuid "8a181e36-5fec-5c45-9657-66ff3657d6a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343440} {:answer/id #uuid "c39755ce-fc8d-515c-bacb-72a25ce9a67d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343959}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343959} :answer-field/id #uuid "87362288-9f29-50fe-9150-93bef3fb4a45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5897420e-efe0-5c2e-8d46-6165bb9e5dd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-76616" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ along the polar curve $r=2\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$  \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi /2)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "76874250-4cf6-5cac-b8f3-c61d135f6019" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483435}}] :activity/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Polar Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482802}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497472} :activity/id #uuid "f54f5ad8-3bb5-5eec-b65a-dbed1b53f523" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048694 :topic/id #uuid "b52b86c6-5073-5d60-a17c-32a308489156" :topic/math-academy-id 2638} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497424 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec9ea01-bfeb-5dfa-bb0d-e8dda89e8cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281819} {:answer/id #uuid "2f167cc3-d0d4-5732-aea7-9737587d5446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta -1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282107} {:answer/id #uuid "a46851a8-a830-5300-ba0a-c94605be1ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283122} {:answer/id #uuid "a598ce3d-2796-5960-afd3-75d843fcccbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283132} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c751ad-dde6-5f48-96a4-ca006c0a37ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283278}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283132} :answer-field/id #uuid "f183aa4f-94f5-53a7-8fa6-3240e8f8d306" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "47feb8ed-9de6-5cab-9530-5d8b3448a054" :question/math-academy-id "q-165413" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$, where $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "167e09bf-7031-5c07-aef8-581424ee1431"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497442 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cee7c2d-32de-5ecb-b754-ad79568f9d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342741} {:answer/id #uuid "5fee99df-a2f2-53ad-aa42-aeb2b8227900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343052} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9b8c0d-eb50-5a20-b648-4799432e59b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343321} {:answer/id #uuid "817d31a5-4314-5a33-8e83-382ed5fd5388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343350} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7f6c1c-7a72-538c-9708-f7cf3bcb5c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343475} :answer-field/id #uuid "946d4e0c-fe04-5d7a-bb82-af9edb848223" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a681b3bc-0951-5b58-83e9-55d23d5a0373" :question/math-academy-id "q-76688" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{2})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "61e4b9a7-6a75-55d9-96ed-a67877cbb8d8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497424}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497450 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345237 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12b1571f-f5f7-562d-80a0-607aa3516530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342403} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9c1e54-997f-58a0-bd84-3b903059ab31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (3-{cos}^{6}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342870} {:answer/id #uuid "7da69662-1fe5-58e3-b0c0-5897eb5c94d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343322} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd26a22-60c5-5518-ab97-c388fe07fef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344065} {:answer/id #uuid "d1097377-be4e-5b0d-8118-afb24f1f0f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344076}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343322} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d5e348e-6403-58e5-a33c-30c8b85d2e1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d123bbf4-7b8c-5b08-b206-9ffde691a39b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76610" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-2{({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7df67029-a10d-5dce-965d-775d1979bb40" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497442}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497464 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f03a0e-89a7-5b16-bf01-1519355c07d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496747} {:answer/id #uuid "3a018c22-1a77-5e85-97e0-1ac9d6577bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496784} {:answer/id #uuid "97cbc095-95df-53b0-aeb5-aa994a815950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496976} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6f0f30-494e-5978-8c42-1f9efcb271d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497127} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5a577e-b9f2-5c3c-87c6-fb5029ee12ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497197}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496976} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cf0e6db-e5f4-547e-8dad-d9a52bf566a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "284c9b33-1577-5c49-9cce-c96ae365ec69" :question/math-academy-id "q-76608" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}x(1-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=1\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x(1-{y}^{2})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x(1-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a773889b-d594-5730-87d3-566a19243567" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497450}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497472 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d386b2-29ca-55a0-a4f9-2285709e1710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342276} {:answer/id #uuid "0724c421-8073-517d-93f3-1620e786e609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342301} {:answer/id #uuid "39b44baa-ac93-5399-be44-580cc1caf867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342714} {:answer/id #uuid "5e406d1b-7ff9-563d-ad72-bf188c813dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343029} {:answer/id #uuid "e4798884-9668-5922-995d-443c2be3e374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344244}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342301} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ab5eb9-1fc8-5190-8bd2-5881ace73b08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aefe264e-7dab-56be-82be-12f2dc01af9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76345" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bd640546-319a-583f-a987-d6f4f9ca8a1a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497464}}] :activity/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Polar Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496658}]}
